ith non-negative coefficients summing to \u221a(2n). We wish to minimize:\n\nJ(f) = 2n * max_{0\u2264i<2n-1} (f * f)[i] / (\u2211_jf_j^2)^2\n\nwhere * denotes the linear convolution.\n\nFor optimization, we parameterize the solution using normalized vectors, making the problem invariant to scale. We convert the minimax problem into a constrained binary optimization using the structure of the provided LP approach.\n\nWe'll use the following optimizations:\n\n1.Linear Programming (LP) Best Response: Find a sequence g(x) that maximizes the sum of coefficients subject to ||f*g||_\u221e \u2264 M, where M is taken from the current maximum convolution value. Then update the sequence with a weighted average.\n\n2.Perhaps should use the full-gradient approach from the subgradient_descent function.\n\n3.Coordinate Descent: Update one coefficient at a time in the direction that minimizes J, ensuring all solutions remain non-negative.\n\nWe must be cautious: the objective function is non-convex, and exact minimization requires iteration.\n\nSection 3: Implementation procedure\n\nThe implementation will consist of the following steps:\n\n1.Setup\n   -Access global variable 'height_sequence_1' (if exists) to use as parent\n   -Create a list of candidate lengths [256, 512]\n   -Use resampling if parent is available to extend to 512\n\n2.Optimization over these lengths:\n\n   For each length, we'll perform multiple phases:\n\n   a.LP Initialization:\n      -Set initial winding to 200% execution time (increase from 50 to 90 seconds)\n      -Increase the maximum iterations (from 5 to 20)\n      -Decrease tighten rate (from 0.01 to 0.005)\n      -Use the provided `run_lp_opt` function but with adjusted time budget\n\n   b.Full-gradient refinement (subgradient_descent):\n      -Set time budget to 200 seconds with adaptive learning rates\n\n   c.Coordinate descent polish:\n      -Try 300 steps or until improvement stall\n\n   d.Monte Carlo simulated annealing:\n      -Use 4.0 restarts with tighter bounds and longer iterations\n\n   However, we must include fallback mechanisms.\n\n3.The core optimization algorithm will be:\n\n   attempted_construction = None\n   best_construction = None\n   best_objective_value = float('inf')\n\n   We'll implement multi-phase optimization for each length candidate:\n\n   while total_time_remaining >= 30:\n      launch_phase_for_length(n)\n\n   But quickly we realize this requires nested function definitions.\n\n   Alternatively, we can use:\n\n   def __search_for_lengths(lengths_list, time_budget):\n        for n in lengths_list:\n            sample_init = generate_inits(n, np.random.default_rng())\n            for init in sample_init:\n                sample_init_candidate = init.copy()\n                # Run the bulk of the optimization on this candidate\n                sample_init_candidate = optimization_kernel(sample_init_candidate)\n                # Evaluate and compare\n\n   Due to complexity, we follow the existing structure but make small incremental changes.\n\n   Actually, I decide to use the following fallback structure:\n\n   if no candidate by deadline:\n        Use candidate = (iterate_index)th element of sample_init\n\n   This ensures we always return something.\n\n4.We'll use the provided `ScoreTracker` to keep the best candidate.\n\n5.The fallback response is:\n\n   if no suitable candidate found:\n        return [0.0] * (256)  # Fallback to a constant sequence (if little time remains)\n\n6.LP Best Response Handling:\n\n   We'll slightly modify the Linprog calls to use an optimized version:\n\n   -Limit the number of inactive constraints\n   -Use dual variables\n   -Terseize any sparse solutions\n\n   But for implementation simplicity, I'll reuse the provided code.\n\n7.We must not create any 0.0 valued coefficients because the evaluate_sequence() function uses defensive clipping but the objective is defined properly.\n\nSection 4: Search choices and variations\n\nStrategically, we'll use:\n\n  