must economize.\n\n        Better estimates might be needed, but we rely on the provided timing.\n\nSection 6: Validation and return contract\n\nWe must ensure:\n\n   4. The returned coefficients are normalized (non-negative, sum\u2248sqrt(2n)) or 1000? But the evaluation function clips to [0,1000].\n\n   We'll return [a0, a1, ..., a_{n-1}] for n=512.\n\n   But the best construction might be shorter if we find it.\n\n   The ScoreTracker is updated and the final candidate is taken from the best solution.\n\nTo summarize, the plan is to use the provided code's optimization framework but with:\n\n   -Increased iterations for all phases\n   -Longer time allocation per length candidate\n   -Multiple restart schedules\n   -Adaptive tightening factors\n   -Fallback mechanisms ensuring valid output\n\nThe implementation will reuse nearly all the helper functions from the provided code, only modifying the time allocation and iteration counts.\n\nAll components must be defined thoroughly within this strategy section.\n\nOne important change will be to allow the search to react to both the provided evaluation function and its inherent difficulty.\n\nBut note: the problem constraints require explicit handling to not exceed 1500 seconds.\n\nLet's proceed with the implementation.",
